"How do bones turn to stone?": "e9b6a6b91096",
"In a museum you may have seen the bones of an animal that died long before there were any people. How can a bone last that long? The truth is that it did not. What you saw was a fossil, a copy of the bone made of stone. And it took a very special chain of events to make it.": "ad962edc3863",
"In a museum you may have seen the bones of an animal that died long before there were any people. How can a bone last that long? The truth is that it did not. What you saw was a fossil, a copy of the bone made of stone. And it took a very special chain of events to make it. It starts with a quick burial. When an animal dies, other animals usually eat it, and what is left rots away. To be saved, the body must be covered up soon, and this happens most easily under water, where mud and sand are always settling. Even then the soft parts rot. Usually only the hard parts, such as bones, teeth, and shells, are left. Then come the layers. Year after year, more mud and sand settle on top. Their weight presses the lower layers together until they harden into rock. In a pile of layers that has not been turned over, the oldest layer is always at the bottom and the youngest is at the top. Inside the rock, something slow and quiet happens. Water seeps through the ground, and it carries dissolved minerals. These minerals fill the tiny spaces inside the bone, and little by little they take the place of the bone itself. In the end, the bone has kept its shape, but it has turned to stone. Now the fossil is deep underground, where nobody can see it. It may stay there forever. But sometimes the land is slowly pushed up, and the old seabed becomes a hill or a mountain. Then rain and rivers begin to wear the rock away, and after a very long time the fossil reaches the surface. That is why Taiwan has fossils. Much of the island was once under the sea, and its rocks have been lifted and worn away. In the hills of Zuozhen, in Tainan, heavy rain often washes fossils out of the banks of the Cailiao River. People there have found the teeth and bones of elephants, deer, and even a rhinoceros that lived in Taiwan hundreds of thousands of years ago.": "efc29b61694f",
